<StatePage title="Mechanics Lien Release: Recorded Claim Discharge Checklist" description="Learn how to identify and discharge a recorded mechanics lien after settlement. Compare full and partial releases, execution, filing, and payment waivers." canonical="https://mechanicslienform.com/mechanics-lien/release/"><article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12 prose prose-lg"><nav aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/mechanics-lien/">Document guides</a></nav>
<h1>Mechanics lien release: clear the specific recorded claim</h1><p>A release or discharge addresses an existing recorded or filed lien. It should identify the claim being cleared and whether the discharge is full or partial. Preparing a document is only part of the task: complete the required execution and filing, then preserve evidence that the records reflect the result.</p>
<h2>Locate the original instrument before drafting</h2><p>Gather the recorded claim, county and office, instrument or book/page number, recording date, claimant, owner, property description, and any amendments or assignments. For a court-filed claim, obtain the correct court docket or filing information. Match the release with the current holder of the claim rather than merely the business name on an old invoice.</p><p>A release that identifies a different parcel or omits an amendment may leave a title problem unresolved. Compare the description with the original claim and settlement documents. Keep a note of any discrepancy and resolve it before signature.</p>
<h2>Full and partial releases need different scope</h2><p>A full discharge generally clears the identified lien. A partial document may address only an amount, parcel, or defined part of the claim. Write the settlement terms and reserved rights clearly and obtain review where a dispute remains. Do not use a full-discharge form for a negotiated partial payment without examining the legal effect.</p><p>Keep the payment amount, payment date, cleared-funds evidence, and settlement agreement in the same file. An unconditional release can surrender security even when a promised payment has not arrived. If payment and release occur through escrow, have the arrangement reviewed and follow its release conditions.</p>
<h2>Check execution and the correct filing office</h2><ol><li>Identify the signer and authority to act for the claimant or assignee.</li><li>Check state execution requirements and local acknowledgment or notarization procedures.</li><li>Confirm the office that holds the original claim and its current submission rules.</li><li>Submit the discharge and retain the accepted or recorded copy.</li><li>Provide copies to settlement participants as agreed and confirm indexing when appropriate.</li></ol><p>Do not assume every state requires the same oath, deadline, or fee. A county recorder workflow also does not describe every state's process: <a href="/mechanics-lien/north-carolina/">North Carolina real-property claims</a> are filed with a court clerk.</p>
<h2>Payment discharge, expiration, and bonding are separate routes</h2><p>A negotiated discharge after payment differs from a court proceeding to remove an expired or invalid lien. It also differs from substituting a bond or other security. Each route has its own eligibility, amount, notices, and procedure. This guide does not provide a universal release deadline or bond multiplier.</p><p>For example, California Civil Code section 8480 addresses a petition concerning a lien whose enforcement period has expired. Michigan MCL 570.1117 addresses bond-based discharge. Neither procedure should be treated as an ordinary payment-release form. Obtain counsel before selecting a contested or bond-based route.</p>
<h2>Keep the underlying state file accessible</h2><p>Follow the applicable claim guide for <a href="/mechanics-lien/illinois/">Illinois</a>, <a href="/mechanics-lien/new-york/">New York</a>, <a href="/mechanics-lien/florida/">Florida</a>, <a href="/mechanics-lien/north-carolina/">North Carolina</a>, or <a href="/mechanics-lien/colorado/">Colorado</a>. Use the <a href="/mechanics-lien/">directory</a> for other states. Keep claim, notice, service, litigation, settlement, and discharge records connected by the same project identifier.</p>
<h2>Do you need a payment waiver instead?</h2><p>A <a href="/lien-waiver/conditional/">conditional lien waiver</a> addresses rights in connection with payment, often before any recorded claim exists. A discharge clears a specified existing claim. Review both tasks when payment arrives, and use the <a href="/mechanics-lien/form/">form checklist</a> to reconstruct missing instrument information.</p><h2>Official references</h2><ul><li><a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?sectionNum=8480.&amp;lawCode=CIV">California Civil Code 8480</a></li><li><a href="https://www.legislature.mi.gov/Laws/MCL?objectName=mcl-570-1117">Michigan MCL 570.1117</a></li><li><a href="https://www.flsenate.gov/Laws/Statutes/2026/713.21">Florida Statutes 713.21 — discharge routes</a></li></ul><p>General information only. Obtain state-specific legal advice before releasing security or contesting a claim.</p></article></StatePage>